{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And your carcasses, you, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say this after He had already said ״in this wilderness your carcasses shall fall.״ And it is possible that the intention is to say that they shall not bring their children's bones into the land, just as they brought up the bones of Joseph and the bones of the tribes into the land, but rather they shall fall in the wilderness." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that the intention is to say that in addition to the fact that their carcasses shall fall as He said above — meaning that dead carcasses shall fall — He further said that the bodies would also have a נְפִילָה (fall) in the wilderness, that they would be in the hand of Samael, prince of the wilderness, for there is no greater נְפִילָה than this, as is known to those who know the esoteric wisdom. And therefore the verse was precise in saying ״and your carcasses, you, shall fall,״ for after calling them פְּגָרִים (carcasses) He said יִפְּלוּ (shall fall), and if He were speaking of death He should have placed the נְפִילָה before applying to them the name פֶּגֶר (carcass), as He said above ״your carcasses shall fall.״ Rather, it is certain that He is speaking of the degradation and fall of the bodies after death. And the verse was precise in saying <b>you</b>, so that one would not understand the statement ״your carcasses״ to refer to their children's children, for the son is a portion of his father; therefore He said ״you.״" "<b>And the sages of</b> Castile, the exiles — I heard from their mouths that they explained that it refers back to Moses and Aaron, that HaShem decreed upon them as well that they would die in the wilderness. And therefore He said the word <b>you</b> to Moses and Aaron, with whom He is speaking. And HaShem gave a reason for His words in what He concluded saying ״if not, I will do this to all this assembly, etc. they shall be consumed and there they shall die,״ (Numbers 14:35) — according to their saying, of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 19:13) on the verse ״And the heads of the people gathered,״ that the reason Moses was buried across the Jordan was to bring the wilderness generation into the World to Come, and therefore if Moses and Aaron had not died in the wilderness the wilderness generation would not have lived. And this is by way of רֶמֶז (hint), for certainly the word פֶּגֶר (carcass) would not be said of Moses and Aaron, whose bodies were pure as angels' — nor would the language of נְפִילָה (fall) be said of the two lights of the world."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {2 "The citation ״וַיֵּתֶא רָאשֵׁי עָם״ appears to be from Deuteronomy 33:5, though the Numbers Rabbah reference (19:13) addresses Moses' burial; the verse quotation may reflect a variant or associative reading in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:09:38.003140320Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}